<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Bautzen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1018">1018</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-polish-war-1002-1018">German–Polish War (1002–1018)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> was signed on 30 January 1018 between Emperor Henry II and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, ending fifteen years of intermittent war. Poland kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> and Milsko, and later that year German knights joined Bolesław's expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-bautzen.jpg" alt="Peace of Bautzen" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julian Nyča · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Budy%C5%A1in_%E2%80%93_pomnik_za_Budyski_m%C4%9Br.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of Emperor Otto III, Bolesław I had backed Eckard I, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, for the succession against Duke Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. When Eckard was murdered at Pöhlde on 30 April 1002, Bolesław took Meissen and the march of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>. At a Hoftag in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> on 25 July 1002 Henry, now King Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, gave him Lusatia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> in return for allegiance and the return of Meissen. Bolesław was ambushed as he left the meeting, and Henry neither protected him nor punished the attackers.</p><p>In 1003 Bolesław conquered the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and allied with German nobles opposed to Henry, who in turn allied with the pagan Lutici. A peace near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a> in 1005, mediated by Tagino, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, cost Poland Lusatia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. Fighting resumed in 1007, and a second peace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> in 1013 restored Lusatia to Bolesław as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, sealed by the marriage of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-lambert">Mieszko II</a> to Richeza, a niece of Otto III.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Bolesław did not help Henry's Italian campaign and instead supported the antipope Gregory VI. In July 1015 Henry renewed the war with his Lutician allies, but Bolesław kept the imperial armies from joining, and Margrave Gero and two hundred German knights were killed by Polish bowmen. In 1017 Henry found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> too strongly fortified and besieged Niemcza without success; the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg">Thietmar of Merseburg</a>, generally ill-disposed towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, praised the defenders' bravery. Henry declared Bolesław a public enemy, and at the end of 1017 Polish troops invaded the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulde">Mulde</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> rivers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Thietmar gave no precise conditions for Bolesław's hold on the Lusatian lands. The German historian Schneidmüller held that they were an imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, the Polish historian Paweł Jasienica that they carried no obligation, and the Cambridge Medieval History describes purely nominal terms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>. Thietmar summed up the peace as not as it should have been but as was possible. Hostages were exchanged, Henry did not attend, and the peace was confirmed by Bolesław's fourth marriage, to Oda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, daughter of Eckard I. Henry promised three hundred knights for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> expedition, and he never renewed the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peace of Bautzen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emperor Henry II and Duke Bolesław I the Brave of Poland concluded the <b>Peace of Bautzen</b>, ending fifteen years of intermittent war over Lusatia, Meissen and Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-30">30 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Boleslaw I fought the emperor Henry II for years, gaining Lusatia at the <b>Peace of Bautzen</b> in 1018, and the same year briefly took Kiev.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-poland">Duchy of Poland</a></li></ul></section>
